  abstract = {Abstract Let \$\$\{\textbackslash mathfrak \{M\}\}\_0\$\$ M 0 be an affine Nakajima quiver variety, and let \$\$\{\textbackslash mathcal \{M\}\}\$\$ M be the corresponding BFN Coulomb branch. Assume that \$\$\{\textbackslash mathfrak \{M\}\}\_0\$\$ M 0 can be resolved by the (smooth) Nakajima quiver variety \$\$\{\textbackslash mathfrak \{M\}\}\$\$ M . The Hikita-Nakajima conjecture claims that there should be an isomorphism of (graded) algebras \$\$H\textasciicircum *\_\{S\}(\{\textbackslash mathfrak \{M\}\},\{\textbackslash mathbb \{C\}\}) \textbackslash simeq \{\textbackslash mathbb \{C\}\}[\{\textbackslash mathcal \{M\}\}\_\{\{\textbackslash mathfrak \{s\}\}\}\textasciicircum\{\{\textbackslash mathbb \{C\}\}\textasciicircum\textbackslash times \}]\$\$ H S {$\ast$} ( M , C ) {$\simeq$} C [ M s C \texttimes{} ] , where \$\$S \textbackslash curvearrowright \textasciitilde\{\textbackslash mathfrak \{M\}\}\_0\$\$ S ↷ M 0 is a torus acting on \$\$\{\textbackslash mathfrak \{M\}\}\_0\$\$ M 0 preserving the Poisson structure, \$\$\{\textbackslash mathcal \{M\}\}\_\{\{\textbackslash mathfrak \{s\}\}\}\$\$ M s is the (Poisson) deformation of \$\$\{\textbackslash mathcal \{M\}\}\$\$ M over \$\$\{\textbackslash mathfrak \{s\}\}=\textbackslash operatorname \{Lie\}S\$\$ s = Lie S , \$\$\{\textbackslash mathbb \{C\}\}\textasciicircum\textbackslash times \$\$ C \texttimes{} is a generic one-dimensional torus acting on \$\$\{\textbackslash mathcal \{M\}\}\$\$ M , and \$\$\{\textbackslash mathbb \{C\}\}[\{\textbackslash mathcal \{M\}\}\_\{\{\textbackslash mathfrak \{s\}\}\}\textasciicircum\{\{\textbackslash mathbb \{C\}\}\textasciicircum\textbackslash times \}]\$\$ C [ M s C \texttimes{} ] is the algebra of schematic \$\$\{\textbackslash mathbb \{C\}\}\textasciicircum\textbackslash times \$\$ C \texttimes{} -fixed points of \$\$\{\textbackslash mathcal \{M\}\}\_\{\{\textbackslash mathfrak \{s\}\}\}\$\$ M s . We prove the Hikita-Nakajima conjecture for \$\$\{\textbac